# AP Psychology — master IDs 11-xxx plus research expansion. Pending teacher review.
@subject ap-psychology | AP Psychology | AP 心理学 | Psych | pink | 12
@desc Brain, mind and behavior: biological bases, cognition, development, learning, social psychology and health.
@framework Unit titles follow the revised AP Psychology course framework (5 units). Research methods appear as Unit 0.

@unit 0 | Research Methods | 研究方法
- operational definition | 操作性定义 | A precise statement of how a variable will be measured or manipulated | 准确说明变量如何测量或操纵，使研究可重复。 | Defining "stress" as cortisol level in saliva is an operational definition. | 2 | #11-001
- independent variable | 自变量 | The factor the researcher manipulates | 研究者操纵的因素。 | In a caffeine study, the amount of caffeine given is the independent variable. | 1 | #11-002
  vs: dependent variable
- dependent variable | 因变量 | The outcome that is measured | 被测量的结果。 | Reaction time is the dependent variable in the caffeine study. | 1 | #11-003
  vs: independent variable
- random assignment | 随机分配 | Assigning participants to conditions by chance | 用随机方法把参与者分配到各组，使组间差异最小，从而支持因果推论。 | Random assignment allows researchers to infer causation in an experiment. | 2 | #11-004
  vs: random sample
- random sample | 随机样本 | A sample in which every member of the population has an equal chance of being selected | 总体中每个人被选中的机会相同，使结果可推广到总体。 | A random sample increases the generalizability of survey results. | 2 | #11-005
  vs: random assignment
- confounding variable | 混杂变量 | An uncontrolled factor that offers an alternative explanation for results | 未被控制、可能解释结果的其他因素。 | If the treatment group also slept more, sleep is a confounding variable. | 2 | #11-006
- placebo effect | 安慰剂效应 | Improvement caused by expectations rather than the treatment itself | 由于期望而非治疗本身带来的改善。 | A control group receiving a sugar pill helps measure the placebo effect. | 2

@unit 1 | Biological Bases of Behavior | 行为的生物学基础
- neuron | 神经元 | A cell specialized to transmit neural signals | 传递神经信号的细胞，由树突、胞体、轴突组成。 | Sensory neurons carry information from the skin to the spinal cord. | 1 | #11-007
- action potential | 动作电位 | A brief electrical charge that travels down an axon | 沿轴突传导的短暂电信号，遵循全或无原则。 | An action potential occurs only when the threshold is reached. | 2
- neurotransmitter | 神经递质 | A chemical messenger released at a synapse | 在突触释放、在神经元之间传递信息的化学物质，如多巴胺、血清素。 | Low serotonin levels are associated with depression. | 1 | #11-008
- neuroplasticity | 神经可塑性 | The brain's capacity to change its structure and function with experience | 大脑根据经验改变结构和功能的能力。 | After a stroke, neuroplasticity helps other brain areas take over lost functions. | 2 | #11-020
- prefrontal cortex | 前额叶皮层 | The front part of the frontal lobe involved in planning, judgment and impulse control | 负责计划、判断和冲动控制的脑区，青春期仍在发育。 | Teenagers' still-developing prefrontal cortex may contribute to risk-taking. | 2
- circadian rhythm | 昼夜节律 | The roughly 24-hour biological clock that regulates sleep and alertness | 约 24 小时的生物钟，调节睡眠与清醒。 | Jet lag disrupts the circadian rhythm. | 1
- sensation | 感觉 | The process of detecting physical energy from the environment | 感受器接收环境刺激并转化为神经信号的过程。 | Sensation begins when light hits receptors in the retina. | 1
  vs: perception
- perception | 知觉 | The process of organizing and interpreting sensory information | 大脑组织和解释感觉信息的过程。 | Seeing a face in a cloud is a matter of perception. | 1
  vs: sensation

@unit 2 | Cognition | 认知
- working memory | 工作记忆 | A limited-capacity system that temporarily holds and manipulates information | 临时储存并加工信息的有限容量系统。 | Holding a phone number while dialing uses working memory. | 2 | #11-013
  vs: long-term memory
- long-term memory | 长期记忆 | The relatively durable and unlimited storage of information | 相对持久、容量很大的信息储存。 | Rehearsal helps move information into long-term memory. | 1 | #11-014
  vs: working memory
- retrieval cue | 提取线索 | A stimulus that helps access stored memories | 帮助提取已储存记忆的刺激。 | Returning to the classroom where you learned material can serve as a retrieval cue. | 2 | #11-015
- encoding | 编码 | The process of getting information into memory | 把信息转化并存入记忆的过程。 | Deep processing improves encoding. | 1
- confirmation bias | 确认偏误 | The tendency to seek and favor evidence that supports existing beliefs | 倾向于寻找和相信支持自己已有观点的证据。 | Reading only news that agrees with you reflects confirmation bias. | 2 | #11-017
- heuristic | 启发法（经验法则） | A mental shortcut that allows quick judgments | 快速判断的心理捷径，可能导致偏差。 | Judging risk by how easily examples come to mind is the availability heuristic. | 2
  vs: algorithm
- algorithm | 算法 | A step-by-step procedure that guarantees a solution | 保证得到答案的逐步程序，比启发法慢但可靠。 | Trying every combination of a lock is an algorithm. | 2
  vs: heuristic

@unit 3 | Development and Learning | 发展与学习
- classical conditioning | 经典条件作用 | Learning in which a neutral stimulus becomes associated with a meaningful stimulus | 中性刺激与有意义刺激反复配对后，单独出现也能引发反应（巴甫洛夫）。 | A dog salivates at a bell after the bell is paired with food. | 2 | #11-009
  vs: operant conditioning
- operant conditioning | 操作性条件作用 | Learning in which behavior is shaped by its consequences | 行为因其后果（强化或惩罚）而增加或减少（斯金纳）。 | A student studies more after receiving praise for good grades. | 2 | #11-010
  vs: classical conditioning
- reinforcement | 强化 | A consequence that increases the likelihood of a behavior | 增加行为再次出现可能性的后果。负强化是“移除厌恶刺激”，不是惩罚。 | Taking aspirin to remove a headache is negative reinforcement. | 2 | #11-011
  mis: Negative reinforcement means punishment — it means removing something unpleasant to INCREASE a behavior.
  vs: punishment
- punishment | 惩罚 | A consequence that decreases the likelihood of a behavior | 减少行为再次出现可能性的后果。 | Losing phone privileges after missing curfew is punishment. | 2 | #11-012
  vs: reinforcement
- observational learning | 观察学习 | Learning by watching and imitating others | 通过观察和模仿他人而学习（班杜拉波波玩偶实验）。 | Children who watched adults hit the Bobo doll imitated the aggression. | 2
- attachment | 依恋 | An emotional bond between an infant and caregiver | 婴儿与照顾者之间的情感纽带。 | Securely attached infants explore freely when the caregiver is present. | 2

@unit 4 | Social Psychology and Personality | 社会心理与人格
- cognitive dissonance | 认知失调 | Discomfort from holding inconsistent beliefs or behaviors | 态度与行为不一致时产生的不适感，人们常改变态度以减少不适。 | A smoker who knows smoking is harmful may experience cognitive dissonance. | 2 | #11-016
- fundamental attribution error | 基本归因错误 | Overemphasizing personality and underestimating situations when explaining others' behavior | 解释他人行为时高估性格因素、低估情境因素的倾向。 | Assuming a late classmate is lazy, rather than stuck in traffic, is the fundamental attribution error. | 2 | #11-018
- social conformity | 从众 | Adjusting behavior or thinking to match group standards | 为符合群体规范而改变自己的行为或想法（阿希实验）。 | In Asch's study, participants gave wrong answers to match the group. | 2 | #11-019
- obedience | 服从 | Following orders from an authority figure | 服从权威人物的命令（米尔格拉姆实验）。 | Milgram found high levels of obedience to an experimenter. | 2
  vs: social conformity
- bystander effect | 旁观者效应 | The tendency to be less likely to help when other people are present | 在场人数越多，个人提供帮助的可能性越小。 | No one called for help in the crowded station because of the bystander effect. | 2

@unit 5 | Mental and Physical Health | 心理与身体健康
- stress | 压力（应激） | The process by which we perceive and respond to events seen as threatening or challenging | 感知并应对威胁或挑战事件的过程。 | Chronic stress can weaken the immune system. | 1
- coping | 应对 | Efforts to manage stress | 处理压力的方法，分为问题聚焦和情绪聚焦。 | Making a study schedule before exams is problem-focused coping. | 2
- psychological disorder | 心理障碍 | A pattern of thoughts, feelings or behaviors that causes significant distress or impairment | 引起显著痛苦或功能损害的思维、情绪或行为模式。 | The DSM classifies psychological disorders by symptoms. | 2
- cognitive behavioral therapy | 认知行为疗法 | Therapy that changes unhelpful thoughts and behaviors | 通过改变不合理的思维与行为来治疗心理问题的疗法。 | CBT helps patients challenge negative automatic thoughts. | 2

@q psy-u0-001 | UNDERSTAND | CONCEPT | SCENARIO | 0 | 2 | random assignment; random sample
? A researcher flips a coin to decide whether each volunteer gets a new study app or the standard one. This procedure is
=* random assignment, which supports causal conclusions. :: Correct — chance determines the condition.
= random sampling, which supports generalization. :: Volunteers were not randomly selected from a population.
= a confounding variable. :: The coin flip controls for confounds.
= an operational definition. :: An operational definition specifies measurement.
! Random assignment = chance determines condition (causation). Random sampling = chance determines who is in the study (generalization).
!zh 随机分配：随机决定分组（支持因果推论）；随机抽样：随机决定谁参加研究（支持推广）。
@q psy-u3-001 | UNDERSTAND | CONCEPT | SCENARIO | 3 | 2 | reinforcement; punishment
? Every time a student turns in homework, the teacher stops sending reminder emails. The student turns in homework more often. This is
= positive punishment. :: Punishment decreases behavior; here behavior increased.
=* negative reinforcement. :: Correct — an unpleasant stimulus is removed, increasing the behavior.
= positive reinforcement. :: Nothing pleasant was added.
= classical conditioning. :: The behavior is shaped by its consequences.
! Reinforcement increases behavior. "Negative" means something is removed.
!zh 强化使行为增加。“负”表示移除某种刺激（此处移除了令人不快的提醒邮件）。

@pair classical-vs-operant | classical conditioning | operant conditioning
! Classical conditioning links two STIMULI (involuntary responses); operant conditioning links a BEHAVIOR to its CONSEQUENCE (voluntary behavior).
!zh 经典条件作用：两个刺激建立联系（非自主反应）；操作性条件作用：行为与后果建立联系（自主行为）。
s: A :: Feeling anxious when you hear a dentist's drill. :: Stimulus association.
s: B :: Studying more after receiving a bonus for good grades. :: Behavior shaped by reward.
s: A :: A child flinches at a camera flash after it was paired with a loud noise. :: Two stimuli associated.
s: B :: A dog learns to sit to receive a treat. :: Voluntary behavior followed by reward.
@pair reinforcement-vs-punishment | reinforcement | punishment
! Reinforcement INCREASES a behavior; punishment DECREASES it. "Positive" adds a stimulus; "negative" removes one.
!zh 强化增加行为，惩罚减少行为。“正”表示施加，“负”表示移除。
s: A :: Removing chores when grades improve, so grades keep improving. :: Behavior increases.
s: B :: Getting a speeding ticket makes a driver slow down in the future. :: Behavior decreases.
s: B :: Losing recess for talking during class. :: Removing something pleasant to reduce behavior.
s: A :: Praise after answering a question makes a student answer more often. :: Behavior increases.
